00:00:10.565 --> 00:00:11.190
ABBY CAREY: Hi.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.590
My name is Abby Carey, and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.420
for Google Cloud DevTools.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.510
And I'll be talking about
maximizing developer

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.070
productivity with Cloud Code.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.340
So what are some development
challenges developers can face?

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.470
One would be repetitive
context switching.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.070
You might find yourself
bouncing between a quick start

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.260
guide, three documentation
sets, your code

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.840
editor, and your terminal,
and your debugger

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.840
just to get your project up
and going and testing it out.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.490
Then there's getting
started with GCP,

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.184
which can be intimidating.

00:00:40.184 --> 00:00:41.767
You don't know where
to start, and you

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.934
can get overwhelmed by the
docs we throw out at you,

00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:45.990
which is quite a bit of docs.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.790
There's a lot of content.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.310
The products we support
can also be complex,

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.620
a big one being Kubernetes.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.710
And quite a few of our products
require YAML configuration

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:56.340
files.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.650
You probably don't
like dealing with YAML.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.890
You want to focus on your
code, not configurations.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:04.540
So these issues I
mentioned usually

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.210
happen in the inner loop,
seen on the left here.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.160
You're probably familiar
with this process.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.260
This is your
day-to-day as a dev.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.040
You code up a feature, build
it, test it, find a bug,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.620
and then rinse and repeat
over and over again

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.000
until your feature's
looking good

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.950
and you're ready to
get into the outer loop

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.080
and then ship it
out to the world.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.690
But how can we make these
development challenges

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:29.070
a bit better and improve
your productivity?

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.170
Well, that's where
Cloud Code comes in.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.650
Cloud Code is an IDE plugin that
automates development workflows

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.750
for cloud-native applications.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.430
This plugin is
composed of a bunch

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:42.210
of tools that can assist you in
a variety of workflows for GCP.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:46.160
It integrates these tools
right into the UI of your IDE.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.060
And these tools
automate repetitive

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.160
processes seen in
your inner loop.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.160
Overall, it's here
to save you time

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.560
and make you more productive.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.390
And looking back at
this picture again,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:03.280
Cloud Code is all about focusing
on the left-hand side here.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.040
So who can use Cloud Code?

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.630
If you're developing Java,
Python, Go, or Node.js,

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.380
chances are, we got you covered.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.440
We support PyCharm,
IntelliJ, GoLand,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.770
and WebStorm from JetBrains,
as well as the popular Visual

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.010
Studio Code, and more
recently, Cloud Shell Editor,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.550
which allows you to use
Cloud Code in your browser.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.820
So what are Cloud Code's
features at a glance?

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.120
First of all, it lets you
build, push, and deploy services

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.470
to Kubernetes and Cloud Run.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.310
It has a simple UI, so it's
easy to rinse and repeat.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.950
It also allows you to debug
Kubernetes and Cloud Run

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:41.520
services with the same UI of
your IDE that you're used to.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.460
All you got to do is place a
break point and then hit Go,

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.530
and you're debugging your
Kubernetes or Cloud Run

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:47.890
services.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.320
It also handles complex tasks,
like port forwarding, for you.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.630
Then there's our
watch mode feature.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.580
This triggers
rebuilds and redeploys

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.050
when code changes happen.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.810
All you got to do is code
up your change, hit Save,

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.820
and then it triggers that
rebuild and redeploy,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.820
speeding up your
development time overall.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.400
Then there's Cloud Code's
most popular feature, which

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.590
is YAML authoring assistance.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.560
You get YAML snippets,
linting, syntax highlighting,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.540
and on-hover docs for
over 400 resource types.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.460
And finally, there's
easy API discoverability,

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.310
with the ability to quickly
enable and get documentation

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:27.400
that you need right
from your IDE.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.210
So what products does
Cloud Code support?

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.190
We support every
product, in a way,

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:36.790
because Cloud Code has
an API Explorer where

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:40.750
you can enable and get the
docs for all of GCP's APIs.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.890
We also have YAML authoring
support for the big ones,

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.230
like Cloud Build and Kubernetes.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:50.450
And we have UI integration
for three GCP products,

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:53.690
including GKE, Cloud
Run, and Secret Manager,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.230
which means we give them
their own dedicated panels

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.990
within your IDE.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.840
So focusing on Cloud
Code's Cloud Run features,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.640
Cloud Code helps
you ramp up fast

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.470
on Cloud Run with
integrated samples

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.390
that show best practices.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:09.940
You can also locally develop
an emulated Cloud Run

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.820
environment with just a
click and view the logs.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.310
This is great for
catching issues

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:18.070
in a production-like
environment before it actually

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.600
has to go to production.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.870
And once that's ready
and looking good,

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.080
you can deploy to Cloud Run
with just a couple clicks.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.240
And finally, you can
view your services

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.090
and their properties
all from your IDE.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.880
But let's look at
